==============================================================================
1. TWO DIFFERENT TRANSACTIONS
==============================================================================
  TX A — legitimate (this proof really verifies)
     proof  4234 B   +   script  67 B
     key:  4,301 bytes   sha256 b7f71a25d87532134e6295cddc216c82…

  TX B — the attack (invalid proof, empty script)
     proof  4301 B   +   script   0 B
     key:  4,301 bytes   sha256 b7f71a25d87532134e6295cddc216c82…

==============================================================================
2. THE CACHE KEY
==============================================================================
  identical?   True
  both are     4,301 bytes
  same sha256  b7f71a25d87532134e6295cddc216c822ad9b87d…

  Nothing was forged and no cryptography was broken. The boundary between
  'where the proof ends' and 'where the script begins' is not recorded
  anywhere — so moving it 67 bytes produces the identical stream.

==============================================================================
3. WHY THAT IS FATAL HERE
==============================================================================
  The lookup happened BEFORE the cryptography:

      if (cache.contains(key)) return true;      <- answers here
      parse_commitments(...);                    <- never reached
      secp256k1_rangeproof_verify(...);          <- never reached

  TX A is verified honestly once, and its key is stored. Sixty seconds later
  TX B arrives, produces the SAME key, hits the cache and is told "already
  verified" — without one elliptic-curve operation being performed.

==============================================================================
4. THE FIX — length-prefix every field, then hash
==============================================================================
  TX A -> 48d4667a51c839cdf46b4023478883f8245d0e737ef95a2d…
  TX B -> 829c7c3a4a43e476040b7c773b1d9bec138dfe9f06175a17…
  identical?   False

  Four bytes of length in front of each field. That is the whole fix:
  'where does this field end' becomes written down instead of guessed.

  Blockstream shipped it in Elements v23.3.4 as 'hardened range-proof cache keys'.
  The same fix was merged to master on 1 September — five days before the attack —
  but no tagged release carrying it had reached a production node.

